Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "high",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "45.83.12.7", "inferred_role": "external_attacker_source", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:02:11+0200", "last_seen": "2027-07-14T09:27:46+0200"},
    {"ip": "10.108.57.198", "inferred_role": "target_corporate_web_login_portal", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:02:11+0200", "last_seen": "2027-07-14T09:27:46+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Identity Information — Credentials", "technique": "T1589.001", "what_happened": "At 2027-07-14 09:02:11 CEST, Suricata logged the first of a sustained series of HTTP POST requests from data.srcip=45.83.12.7 to data.url=/login on the corporate web login portal data.dstip=10.108.57.198. The requests carried data.http.method=POST with a single recurring data.http.user_agent string ('Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/119.0.0.0 Safari/537.36'). Wazuh decoders parsed each POST as a failed web authentication attempt and the rule engine began counting failures per source. This is the opening of a credential-based attack: the adversary has assembled a credential set (breached-password list cross-referenced against a corporate-format username list) and is testing it against the live login portal.", "chain_reason": "Suricata HTTP event with data.srcip=45.83.12.7 + data.url=/login + data.http.method=POST + rule.description matching 'web authentication failure'. Single source IP, single user-agent, repeated POSTs to the same /login endpoint within a tight window.", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Credential Access / Brute Force — Password Spraying", "technique": "T1110.003", "what_happened": "Between 09:02 and 09:27 CEST, the same data.srcip=45.83.12.7 cycled a SMALL set of common/breached passwords across a LARGE set of distinct usernames — the defining signature of password spraying (as opposed to classic brute force, which hammers one account with many passwords). Each individual account received only a handful of attempts (well below the per-account lockout threshold), but in aggregate the source generated a high volume of failed web logins against many distinct accounts. The usernames followed the corporate firstname.lastname / f.lastname convention (administrator, admin, helpdesk, john.smith, alice.jones, a.dupont, m.lefebvre, etc.), indicating the attacker pre-built the username list from identity reconnaissance. Wazuh fired a correlation rule with rule.mitre.id=T1110.003 (Brute Force: Password Spraying).", "chain_reason": "Wazuh correlation rule 'Multiple failed web authentications across distinct accounts from a single source' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1110.003. Pattern: one data.srcip, many distinct usernames in the POST bodies, low attempts-per-account, failures clustered in a ~25-minute window.", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:04:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Credential Access / Brute Force — Lockout-Evasion Pacing", "technique": "T1110.003", "what_happened": "The spray was deliberately PACED: roughly one attempt every 8 seconds per account, with multi-minute pauses inserted between password rounds (the attacker sprayed password #1 across all usernames, paused ~3 minutes, then sprayed password #2, and so on). This timing keeps the per-account failure count under naive rate-limit and lockout thresholds while still working through the entire user base. The pacing is the operator-skill marker that distinguishes a competent credential-spray operation from a noisy automated brute-force bot. Suricata continued logging the POSTs across the full 09:02-09:27 window and Wazuh aggregated them under the same correlation rule.", "chain_reason": "Temporal analysis of the Suricata HTTP events: data.srcip=45.83.12.7 POSTs to data.url=/login spaced ~8s apart with ~3-minute inter-round gaps. Same data.http.user_agent throughout. The deliberate pacing + round structure is the lockout-evasion signature.", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:10:00+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Credential Access / Cumulative Volume", "technique": "T1110.003", "what_happened": "Across the 09:02-09:27 window (~25 minutes), Wazuh aggregated the failed-web-authentication alerts from data.srcip=45.83.12.7 into a single high-severity correlated incident. The cumulative volume (many distinct usernames, all failing, from one external source, with breached-password-style inputs and lockout-evading pacing) is the canonical password-spraying fingerprint that MITRE T1110.003 describes. As of the last event at 09:27:46 there is NO observed successful authentication in the alert payload — but the spray covered privileged-sounding accounts (administrator, admin, it.admin, security.ops, finance.lead) which raises the stakes of the hunting question 'did any account actually fall?'.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh correlation rule 'High-volume web authentication failures from single external source across multiple accounts' fired on aggregate failure count from same data.srcip within a 30-minute window, MITRE mapping T1110.003 attached automatically.", "first_seen": "2027-07-14T09:27:00+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2027-07-14T09:02:11+0200", "end": "2027-07-14T09:27:46+0200"},
